<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Decimus Junius Brutus Albinus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 85–<a href="/years/43-bce">43 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Decimus Junius Brutus Albinus was a Roman general and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s leading officers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, where in 56 BCE he commanded the fleet that destroyed the navy of the Veneti. Although favoured by Caesar, he joined the conspiracy against him and took part in his assassination in 44 BCE. Besieged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Mutina</a> in 43 BCE, he was relieved but later deserted by his troops and killed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/decimus-junius-brutus-albinus.jpg" alt="Decimus Junius Brutus Albinus" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abel de Pujol · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C%C3%A9sar_se_rendant_au_s%C3%A9nat_aux_Ides_de_Mars.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Decimus Brutus was probably the son of the senator Decimus Junius Brutus and his notorious wife Sempronia, a participant in the conspiracy of Catilina in 63 BCE. His father, grandfather and great-grandfather had all been consuls. He was adopted by a patrician named Postumius Albinus, and some ancient sources and his own coins call him Albinus, but he seems not to have changed his name, and his contemporaries used his birth name.</p><p>Caesar said on several occasions that he loved him like a son, and named him an heir in the second degree in his will and guardian of any child he might have. He is often confused with his distant cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-junius-brutus">Marcus Junius Brutus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He served in Caesar's army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a> and in 56 BCE commanded the fleet against the Veneti. In the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morbihan">Morbihan</a> he destroyed their ships by attacking their sails with sickle-like hooks fixed on long poles, leaving them helpless for Roman boarding parties. He also served against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> in 52 BCE. In the civil war he sided with Caesar and was again entrusted with fleet operations: left in charge of the naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of Massilia, he built a fleet from scratch within thirty days, defeated the Massilian fleet twice and, with Gaius Trebonius, secured the city's surrender.</p><p>In 44 BCE Caesar made him Praetor Peregrinus and designated him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a> for the next year. He helped persuade Marcus Brutus to join the conspiracy against Caesar. On the Ides of March he persuaded Caesar to attend the Senate, escorted him there and kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> away; according to Nicolaus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> he struck Caesar through the thigh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Antony opposed Decimus's claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a>, and when the Senate ordered him to give up the province he refused. In 43 BCE he occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Mutina</a>, and Antony blockaded him. The consuls Aulus Hirtius and Gaius Pansa marched to relieve the siege with Octavian, and Antony was defeated at the battles of Forum Gallorum on 14 April and at Mutina on 21 April.</p><p>Octavian coldly told Decimus he had come to oppose Antony, not to help Caesar's murderers, and many of Decimus's soldiers deserted to him. He fled across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, was left exposed when Plancus switched sides and was executed in mid-September by Camelus, a Gallic chief loyal to Antony. Several of his letters survive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>'s correspondence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Decimus Junius Brutus Albinus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Decimus Brutus</b> defeated them in a calm, probably in Quiberon Bay, by cutting their rigging with hooked poles.</blockquote><a href="/state/veneti">Veneti</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-58" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Decimus Junius Brutus Albinus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-58">What was here in 58 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
